<%# THE MONEY COLUMN — Home's first two answers as three stat tiles (two-shapes spec §3).
    Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter). It replaces `home/_hero.html.erb`.

    ** WHAT MOVED AND WHAT DID NOT. ** The card's THREE FIGURES and both of its SENTENCES are §2's,
    unchanged and pinned by the examples `hero_spec.rb` carried into `money_spec.rb`: in checking,
    free = pot − Σ claims, the claimed/parked subline, and the overdraft line. What changed is the
    shape — three tiles rather than a definition list — and what LEFT is the period bar: a period is
    the runway's subject now (`_runway.html.erb`), and drawing it twice on one screen was how the
    "7 days left" sentence came to be printed by two panels that could disagree.

    ** THE BAR IS THE ONE NEW THING, AND IT IS THE SUBLINE AS A PICTURE. ** Two segments of the pot,
    free and claimed, off ONE figure (`#claimed_percent`) so they cannot add up to something that is
    not the whole. It is drawn on the free tile because free is the number it explains.

    THE WORDS ARE THE USER'S (answers-first §3): in checking, free, claimed, other accounts. "Set
    aside", "spoken for", "available", "goal" and "fund" are not on this screen — nothing is moved
    anywhere and nothing holds money; a rule CLAIMS money where it sits. %>
<div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded p-4 sm:p-6 space-y-4" data-money>
  <%# TILE 1 — THE NUMBER THE BANK APP SHOWS. `#in_checking` is `AccountLedger#pot`: main's balance,
      not the user's money everywhere, because that is the figure they can check against their phone.
      RED BELOW ZERO — a physical overdraft is money that has already gone, which no purpose-side
      figure on this screen can say. %>
  <div class="rounded border border-gray-200 px-4 py-3">
    <p class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">In checking</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-2xl font-semibold tabular-nums <%= presenter.in_checking.negative? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>"
       data-in-checking>
      <%= number_to_currency(presenter.in_checking) %>
    </p>
    <%# THE FACT ALONE, AND NO CONSEQUENCE (fix round 1 — MED-1). This ended "— nothing is free until
        money comes in", which is a lie for the user whose money is simply in the wrong account.
        Which of the two negative-pot states it is belongs to the subline, which is the one place on
        this card that knows. %>
    <% if presenter.in_checking.negative? %>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-status-danger" data-checking-overdrawn>
        Your checking account is already spent past zero.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# TILE 2 — THE ONE DERIVED NUMBER: `pot − Σ claims` (§2). The sage tile, because it is the answer
      the user came for and the only figure on this screen their bank cannot tell them. Never
      clamped: a negative free renders negative, in red (§4 — it is a signal, not a refusal). %>
  <div class="rounded border border-brand bg-brand-light px-4 py-3">
    <p class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-brand-dark">Free to spend</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-3xl font-semibold tabular-nums <%= presenter.free_to_spend.negative? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-brand-dark" %>"
       data-free>
      <%= number_to_currency(presenter.free_to_spend) %>
    </p>

    <%# THE TWO SEGMENTS, of checking: what is free and what the rules claim. Drawn only where the
        pot is a quantity to be a fraction of — an empty or overdrawn account is not, and a bar there
        would have to invent a denominator (`#claimed_percent` is nil, and this renders nothing).

        THE CLAIMED SEGMENT IS THE ONE THAT CARRIES THE HOOK AND THE WIDTH, deliberately: at 0% a fill
        has no width and Capybara does not consider it visible, so the percent rides on the track —
        `_this_period`'s own rule, and the app's other bars draw the same way. %>
    <% if claimed = presenter.claimed_percent %>
      <div class="mt-3 h-2 bg-white rounded-full overflow-hidden"
           role="progressbar"
           aria-valuenow="<%= claimed %>"
           aria-valuemin="0"
           aria-valuemax="100"
           aria-label="<%= number_to_currency(presenter.total_claims) %> of checking claimed by your rules"
           data-claimed-bar="<%= claimed %>">
        <div class="h-2 <%= presenter.short? ? "bg-status-danger" : "bg-brand-darker" %>"
             style="width: <%= claimed %>%;"
             data-claimed-fill></div>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# ** THE SUBLINE'S TWO ARMS (§2), VERBATIM FROM TASK 1. ** Each half is gated on the predicate
        that ESTABLISHES it rather than inferred from the signs of two figures:

          claimed, money elsewhere   → "Your rules claim $X more…" + "Move some in…"
          claimed, one account       → "Your rules claim $X more…" + "You have spent past what you had."
          nothing claimed, elsewhere → "Move some in…"   (the pot is under; the money exists)
          nothing claimed, one acct  → "You have spent past what you had."

        ** THE SECOND CLAUSE IS ABOUT PARKED MONEY AND NOTHING ELSE (fix wave — LOW-1). ** It was
        gated `elsif !anything_claimed?`, which made "You have spent past what you had" unreachable
        for the user it is truest of: free below zero, rules claiming, and no other account to move
        anything in from. That user got the shortfall figure and then silence about what it means.
        §2 draws the line where the MONEY is — there is somewhere to move it from, or there is not —
        so the else takes the spec's clause. `_shortfall.html.erb`'s HEADLINE still splits on
        `#anything_claimed?`, which is a different question (whose fault the gap is), and that split
        stays. %>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm <%= presenter.free_to_spend.negative? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-brand-dark" %>"
       data-free-subline>
      <% if presenter.free_to_spend.negative? %>
        <% if presenter.anything_claimed? %>
          Your rules claim <%= number_to_currency(presenter.shortfall) %> more than checking holds.
        <% end %>
        <% if presenter.money_parked_elsewhere? %>
          Move some in from your other accounts.
        <% else %>
          You have spent past what you had.
        <% end %>
      <% elsif presenter.money_parked_elsewhere? %>
        <%# ONE SENTENCE, NOT TWO: joined with ", and" so the second fact reads as part of the first
            rather than as an afterthought. %>
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.total_claims) %> of checking is claimed by your rules, and
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.other_accounts_total) %> sits in
        <%= pluralize(presenter.other_accounts.count, "other account") %>.
      <% else %>
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.total_claims) %> of checking is claimed by your rules.
      <% end %>
    </p>
  </div>

  <%# TILE 3 — MONEY THAT IS NOT IN CHECKING (§2): shown, never subtracted from or added to anything.
      A SMALLER FIGURE THAN THE TWO ABOVE, because it is not an answer to "what can I spend" — it is
      the fact that closes the shortfall arm's instruction, and the chips say which accounts hold it.

      ABSENT WHERE THERE IS NOTHING TO SAY. `#other_accounts` empty is a user with one account, and a
      tile reading "$0.00 in 0 other accounts" would be the app inventing an absence — the same
      refusal `_accounts_line` makes about its own summary. %>
  <% if presenter.other_accounts.any? %>
    <div class="rounded border border-gray-200 px-4 py-3" data-other-accounts>
      <p class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">
        In <%= pluralize(presenter.other_accounts.count, "other account") %>
      </p>
      <p class="mt-1 text-lg font-semibold tabular-nums text-gray-900" data-other-accounts-total>
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.other_accounts_total) %>
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
        <% presenter.other_accounts.each do |account| %>
          <li class="rounded bg-gray-100 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs text-gray-700" data-account-chip="<%= account.name %>">
            <%= account.name %>
          </li>
        <% end %>
      </ul>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
